In article <ykhdbdrg@csv.warwick.ac.uk> ecuwk@warwick.ac.uk (Huggybear) writes:
>In article <1992Jan30.173134.19436@schaefer.math.wisc.edu> mckinzie@math.wisc.edu writes:
>>Did you know you can answer
>>     Which sorts of things would you like identified[)[("+= a]?
>>with more than one argument?  So e.g. if we do
>>
>>Which sorts of things would you like identified[)[("+= a]?   =+()["
>>
>>it will first do your ring(s), then if it can continue, it will
>>continue onto the spellbooks, then the tools, etc...
>
> Another way is to reply something like 'i', so it says you have no i's,
>and then it goes through your inventory item by item...this may be a little
>easier than working out your priority of identifying things...

True, but if the ring which just must get identified is V, and there are
three things before it (the order in which stuff is identified is a-zA-Z),
then you must sit there hitting 'n' and 'y' for what will seem like hours and,
if you are counting on the scroll identifying three things and it stops after
two, the ring just won't get identified.  So prioritizing is very useful 
unless nothing is at all important.

A warning: if you reply 'i' and you do have item 'i', you will only identify
that one object and no others.  So be careful if you respond with a letter
(especially a or A, which I have hit thinking 'ALL' and finding out that my
blessed +1 athame was a blessed +1 athame).

